{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Ham, the father of Canaan</b> — Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 9:18) that since the passage is occupied with and deals with Noah's drunkenness, in which Ham sinned and through which Canaan was cursed, and the genealogy of Ham had not yet been written, it was necessary to say that Ham was the father of Canaan. And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra) said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 9:24) that Ham saw and told his brothers, while Canaan did something evil to him [Noah] that the verse did not disclose. And this is the meaning of \"that which his youngest son had done to him,\" for Canaan is the youngest of Ham's sons, as when Scripture enumerates them: \"And the sons of Ham: Cush, and Mizraim, and Put, and Canaan\" (Genesis 10:6). But behold, Rabbi Abraham has abandoned his usual way with the plain sense of Scripture and begun to prophesy falsehoods. And the correct view in my eyes is that Ham is the youngest of Noah's sons, as I explained at the beginning of the portion (Ramban on Genesis 6:10), and Canaan is the eldest son of Ham, and when Scripture says \"And the sons of Ham: Cush, and Mizraim, and Put, and Canaan,\" after he was sold as a servant of servants, it placed all his brothers above him. And when this incident happened to Noah, Ham had no offspring other than Canaan, and this is the meaning of \"And Ham, the father of Canaan, saw\" (Genesis 9:22), for he had no other son; and when he sinned against his father, he [Noah] cursed his offspring. For if he had said, \"Cursed be Ham; a servant of servants shall he be,\" it would harm only his own person, since offspring already born are not included in it, and perhaps he would not beget children, and he would not take vengeance upon him — for who knows what will come after him? Therefore he cursed the son who had been born to him, and if he begets a hundred, it is enough that his firstborn son and all his offspring with him are cursed. Now the sin was that Ham saw his father's nakedness and did not treat him with honor, for it was fitting for him to cover his nakedness and conceal his shame, so as not to tell it even to his brothers; but he told the matter to his two brothers in public, to mock him — and this is the meaning of \"outside,\" and so Onkelos translated: בְּשׁוּקָא (\"in the marketplace\"). And the meaning of \"and he knew what his youngest son had done to him\" is that he [Ham] had exposed his shame to the public, and he [Noah] was ashamed of the matter. And our Sages added a further sin upon him (see Sanhedrin 70a)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:45.957783127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}